CLAIMS=[
('Investing $500/month at 8% takes about 12 years to reach $100,000; of that, ~$84,000 is contributions and ~$16,000 returns.','approximately 12 years of consistent monthly contributions',C+'Monthly compounding at 8%/12: $100,000 is reached after 10.6 years (127.6 months); after 12 years the balance is $120,254. Contributions at $100,000 are ~$64,000 (12 years = $72,000); $84,000 corresponds to 14 years. Returns at $100,000 are ~$36,000, not $16,000. The split is wrong.',[]),
('It takes only ~6 more years to double to $200,000; "we are now 20 years in" at $200,000 with ~$120,000 contributed and ~$80,000 growth.','It only takes about 6 more years to double',C+'$200,000 is reached at 16.3 years (5.7 years after $100,000, so "about 6 more" is right). But 12 + 6 = 18, not 20; at 20 years the balance is $294,510 with $120,000 contributed and $174,510 growth. The script mixes the 16-18-year balance with the 20-year contributions.',[]),
('At $200,000 you are "more than halfway" to $1 million.','you\'re actually more than halfway',C+'In time: $200,000 at 16.3 years vs $1M at 33.4 years, so 16.3/33.4 = 49% of the time; "about halfway" in time is defensible, not "more than halfway". In dollars it is 20%. The claim is framed loosely.',[]),
('By 35 years you have over $1 million from ~$210,000 contributed, more than 75% from growth; returns ~$80,000 a year.','By the time you hit the 35- year mark',C+'35 years: $1,146,941 balance, $210,000 contributed (18.3%), growth 81.7%: verified. 8% of the balance is ~$91,800/year ($80,000 is 8% of exactly $1M).',[]),
('By year 40 you have over $2 million, generating more than $160,000 a year.','By year 40, you\'re looking at over $2 million',C+'40 years: $1,745,504 (annual compounding gives $1.55M). $2M is not reached until ~41.6 years; 8% of $1.75M is ~$140,000, not $160,000. Overstated.',[]),
('The 8% return is already inflation-adjusted and conservative; the market historically returned closer to 10%.','is actually adjusted for inflation',R+'Damodaran S&P 500 1928-2024: ~10% nominal geometric, ~7% real (recall; page unreachable this session). If 8% is real, it is above the ~7% historical real return, so it is optimistic, not conservative. The script also earlier called 8% "conservative compared to historical performance", contradicting itself on whether the figure is real or nominal.',[]),
('The average household spends over $700/month on food; cutting 30% frees over $200.','spends over $700 per month on food',P+'BLS Consumer Expenditures release (Wayback capture of USDL-25-1586): 2023 food $9,985/year ($832/month) and 2024 $10,169 ($847/month). The 2024 release came after the upload; the 2023 value (first published September 2024) was available. "Over $700" is true. '+C+'30% of $832 = $250, consistent with "over $200".',['BLSCE276']),
('The average car payment is now over $800/month.','The average car payment in America is now over $800',SN+'EDQ325: Edmunds Q3 2025 average new-vehicle payment ~$754 (Q2 $756), with ~19% of new loans at $1,000+ (search summaries; Edmunds page returned 403). "Over $800" overstates the new-car average and far overstates the all-vehicle (used ~$520) average. '+U,['EDQ325']),
('Employer matches of 50 cents per dollar give an instant 50% return; advisers charge 2% annual fees.','match 50 cents for every dollar',R+'A 50% match is a common formula (often up to 6% of pay); the instant-return arithmetic is correct. Typical advisory fees are ~1% of AUM, so "2%" overstates the norm. '+U,[]),
('Compound interest called the eighth wonder of the world (attributed to Einstein).','the eighth wonder of the world',R+'The Einstein attribution is undocumented; the script itself hedges that it may be apocryphal. Accurate hedging.',[]),
]
TITLE='Authentic title "Path From $100K To $1 Million (The Math They Don\'t Teach You)" promises a calculated milestone path with a hidden-knowledge parenthetical. The script delivers a $500/month at 8% walkthrough, but several milestone figures do not reproduce. TYPICAL against the cohort. Association only.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White background with grey floorboards. Left: the recurring cartoon man in a light-blue shirt and dark trousers, smug half-smile, holding out a strapped stack of cash with a small pile at his feet. Right: a heavy black three-line headline "WHERE REAL WEALTH STARTS" and a curved arrow pointing at a green bank-vault door. At 320x180 the headline and vault stay legible. Neither $100K nor $1 Million appears on the image, so the title\'s specific numbers are carried by the title alone.'
SCRIPT='A compound-interest walkthrough: hook ($100K is the starting gun); linear-thinking strawman; eighth-wonder line; $500/month at 8% milestones (12 years to $100K, 6 more to $200K, "20 years", 35 years to $1M, 40 years to $2M); inflation and crashes objections (8% "inflation-adjusted"); budget cuts (food $700, car $800); psychology of a six-figure balance; options and opportunities; rising contributions; culture of instant gratification; employer match; close (pregnant-with-twins line). Verified: 35-year $1.15M from $210K. Wrong: $100K takes 10.6 years and the contribution/returns split ($84K/$16K) is wrong; "20 years at $200K" (actually $294,510); year-40 "over $2M" ($1.75M) and "$160K a year" ($140K); car payment "over $800" (Edmunds ~$754 new). Contradiction: 8% called both conservative and inflation-adjusted. The stored ending is complete.'
